{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# The published head trace: follow a publisher's index HEAD by principal\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: **design proposed 2026-10-05, revision 2 (the simple core);\n     4\tnothing here is implemented.** Author seat: claude-fable-5-1 (Lead System\n     5\tArchitect). Revision 2 rewrites the design to the scope the owner chose:\n     6\tone configured source per principal, one candidate at a time, durable\n     7\tinstalled heads, loopback serving. Relaying, many sources and serving\n     8\toff loopback are section 12's, each its own later design; section 8\n     9\tshows that none of them changes the core. It awaits an independent\n    10\treview and the owner's answers to section 13. Subordinate to\n    11\t[`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md),\n    12\t[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md),\n    13\t[`dao.stream.remote.md`](./dao.stream.remote.md),\n    14\t[`dao.stream.ws.md`](./dao.stream.ws.md),\n    15\t[`dao.jing.dht.md`](./dao.jing.dht.md) and\n    16\t[`yin.vm.linker.dht.md`](./yin.vm.linker.dht.md). In sections 5 to 7\n    17\tevery sentence is a rule.\n    18\t\n    19\t## 1. Objective\n    20\t\n    21\tA publisher deposits its index HEAD as a signed trace on a stream it alone\n    22\twrites. A reader that declares the publisher's principal follows that\n    23\ttrace, loads each index it names, and resolves `(require 'alib)` against\n    24\tthe newest head it has installed. The reader is handed no manifest\n    25\taddress: the join token is `yin:<host:port>/<principal>`.\n    26\t\n    27\tThis is the \"latest-root discovery\" that `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` sections 1\n    28\tand 13 and `dao.jing.dht.md` section 1 (owner decision 3) left to a later\n    29\tepic. It changes nothing in how a name is resolved once an index is\n    30\tloaded, and nothing in the DHT.\n    31\t\n    32\t## 2. The owner's words\n    33\t\n    34\tVerbatim, 2026-10-05:\n    35\t\n    36\t> is it possible to start the yin.repl to always reference whatever the HEAD\n    37\t> is? (require 'alib) should always use the index manifest of the HEAD\n    38\t\n    39\t> publishing a's head is a what stigmergy is for. a can publish its head and\n    40\t> c can read it\n    41\t\n    42\tOn the scope, after two reviews:\n    43\t\n    44\t> option 1 for now, but cross-machine is necessary once option 1 is done.\n    45\t> however,if the abstraction is dao.stream, cross-machine would not require\n    46\t> a complete redesign, right?\n    47\t\n    48\tThe orchestrator's readings (paraphrases, not the owner's words): the\n    49\thead is a trace A deposits in a shared medium and C perceives, not a\n    50\tmutable-pointer service C calls; and \"option 1\" is one configured source\n    51\tper principal, one candidate load at a time with latest-wins, durable\n    52\tinstalled-head persistence, and loopback following.\n    53\t\n    54\tBoth readings hold. Section 8 answers the question in the third quote.\n    55\t\n    56\t## 3. The invariants it rests on\n    57\t\n    58\t- **A host boundary is a stream boundary; a stream has no privileged\n    59\t  reader and its meaning is not in it** (`datom.world.md`, Streams and\n    60\t  Host Boundaries). The head is a value on a ring. The follower reads it\n    61\t  with `cursor` and `next` and nothing else.\n    62\t- **Derive, don't persist** (`datom.world.md`, Design Principles). The\n    63\t  trace's sequence is the greatest transaction `t` of the index it names.\n    64\t- **Sources stay separate db-values; union is explicit**\n    65\t  (`datom.world.md`, Tuples and Datoms). A followed index is never merged\n    66\t  into the reader's own.\n    67\t- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics.** The DHT learns nothing\n    68\t  about roots (`dao.jing.dht.md` section 1).\n    69\t- **Any stream may be exposed over WebSocket or UDP, peer to peer; no\n    70\t  server, no client, no privileged node** (`dao.stream.remote.md`\n    71\t  section 1, owner, 2026-09-25). The board is one mirror-table entry.\n    72\t- **A fetch has no deadline; liveness is the drive's, through\n    73\t  `dao.lease`; the linker is clock-free** (owner, 2026-09-26). No rule\n    74\t  below ends anything by time. Delays are counted in the node's ticks.\n    75\t- **`dao.stream.apply` is independent of rpc** (owner, 2026-09-29). No\n    76\t  apply word is added.\n    77\t- **Plain Clojure first** (owner, 2026-10-01). Section 6 is that path.\n    78\t- **No backward compatibility** (owner). The three-part token goes.\n    79\t- **Single-writer provenance; a trace is deposited, never addressed**\n    80\t  (`dao.agent.md` invariant 2, section 4.3).\n    81\t- **Authority is the reader's composition** (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md`\n    82\t  7.1). A trace is honoured only for a declared principal.\n    83\t\n    84\t## 4. The starting tree\n    85\t\n    86\tRead at `ee3498de`.\n    87\t\n    88\t**Defects in the current design.**\n    89\t\n    90\t- **D1. Joining by hydration forks the publisher's index.** `dht join`\n    91\t  installs the token's manifest as the reader's own HEAD\n    92\t  (`src/cljc/yin/repl/dht.cljc:202-212`); the reader's next round\n    93\t  appends to it, and `refused-head` (`dht.cljc:46-53`) then refuses any\n    94\t  later head. Correction: `dht join` stops hydrating; a followed head is\n    95\t  a snapshot beside the reader's HEAD (5.5). Hydration itself is left\n    96\t  alone (5.8).\n    97\t- **D2. `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 7.2** has no place for a snapshot that\n    98\t  follows a principal. It gains one member (5.5), and sections 1 and 13\n    99\t  drop the latest-root deferral (slice H1).\n   100\t- **D3. `dao.space.dht/load` is silent across kinds.** A load of an\n   101\t  address already recorded under another `:kind` is \"left as it is\"\n   102\t  (`src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc:1166-1170`), so the caller gets a record\n   103\t  that is not what it asked for. One consumer per address hid it. 5.5\n   104\t  makes it a refusal.\n   105\t\n   106\t**Contradictions, stated instead of designed around.**\n   107\t\n   108\t- **C1.** The signature cannot live in `m`: `datom.md` (line 168) fixes\n   109\t  `m` as an integer reference to a metadata entity.\n   110\t- **C2.** A head under a DHT key contradicts `dao.jing.dht.md` section 1.\n   111\t- **C3. A well-known name used as a mirror-table identity is already in\n   112\t  the tree, and the contract does not clearly allow it.** `dao.stream.md`\n   113\t  (Envelopes) makes `:dao.stream/identity` the identity of the sequence\n   114\t  one transport instance owns. Yet `yin.repl` serves two per-process\n   115\t  rings under the fixed strings `\"yin.repl/requests\"` and\n   116\t  `\"yin.repl/answers\"` (`src/cljc/yin/repl/connect.cljc:64-82`,\n   117\t  `src/cljc/yin/repl/serve.cljc:224-225`), \"a stable service name\n   118\t  [that] keeps `attach!` honest across endpoint restarts\". The core\n   119\t  follows that landed convention for the board (5.1) and does not rely\n   120\t  on it: the reader survives any identity answer (5.3). The contract\n   121\t  must say which is right, once, for both; that is amendment A1 (H2),\n   122\t  and it must be settled before relaying (section 8), where one name\n   123\t  would reach many rings.\n   124\t\n   125\t**Gaps.** A loaded index does not survive a restart (the node's\n   126\t`:loads` are process state). A name that moved under a live session is\n   127\tsilent. `announce!` is private and unsigned, correctly (5.4).\n   128\t\n   129\t## 5. The design\n   130\t\n   131\t### 5.1 Medium (decision 1)\n   132\t\n   133\t**The head trace lives on a ring the publisher alone writes. A reader\n   134\treaches it as a `dao.stream` reader handle. That is the whole coupling.**\n   135\t\n   136\t- The **head board** is a `dao.stream.ringbuffer` of capacity 1.\n   137\t- **The follower takes a reader handle per principal and calls only\n   138\t  `cursor` and `next` on it.** It does not know whether the handle is the\n   139\t  ring itself, a reflection over WebSocket, or anything else. Section 8\n   140\t  rests on this sentence.\n   141\t- **Exposure in the core: `dao.stream.ws`, on loopback.** The publisher's\n   142\t  node composes one WebSocket acceptor (`dao.stream.ws-project`) whose\n   143\t  mirror table has one entry, the board, surface `#{:reader}`, under the\n   144\t  name `\"yin.head/ed25519:<64 lowercase hex>\"` (C3). It listens on TCP at\n   145\t  **the same port number as the node's UDP socket**, path `/head`, so one\n   146\t  `host:port` names both and the token needs no second address.\n   147\t- The board has its own endpoint. It is never entered in the REPL\n   148\t  service's table: that table carries `yin.repl/requests`, which is\n   149\t  evaluation.\n   150\t- **Loopback only.** The endpoint is composed only when the node's bind\n   151\t  host is a loopback literal. On any other bind the node serves no\n   152\t  board, prints no token and says so. Section 8 says what lifts this.\n   153\t- **One source per principal**: the address the reader was given. Nothing\n   154\t  is put in the DHT, which is used as today to fetch the blobs.\n   155\t\n   156\t**Availability.** Publisher running: the reader asks once per poll\n   157\tinterval and observes a new head at the first poll that is answered.\n   158\tPublisher stopped: the reader keeps the head it installed and everything\n   159\tit linked. Nothing times out. A reader that never reached its source has\n   160\tno head; 5.6 says what a `require` does then.\n   161\t\n   162\tRejected: UDP on the DHT socket as the first exposure (section 8.3); a\n   163\tDHT key (C2).\n   164\t\n   165\t### 5.2 Trace schema (decision 2)\n   166\t\n   167\t```clojure\n   168\t{:yin.head/envelope {:yin.head/principal \"ed25519:<64 lowercase hex>\"\n   169\t                     :yin.head/manifest  :segment/...\n   170\t                     :yin.head/seq       1042}\n   171\t :yin.head/proof    {:yin.head/signature \"<128 lowercase hex>\"}}\n   172\t```\n   173\t\n   174\t- The key sets are closed; a trace carrying another key is malformed.\n   175\t- No time and no address are written. A trace says nothing about where\n   176\t  it was read, which is what lets any transport, and later any relay,\n   177\t  carry it unchanged.\n   178\t- The signed message is\n   179\t  `UTF-8(\"yin.head/trace:v1\\n\") || dao.jing/canonical-bytes(envelope)`.\n   180\t  The prefix is domain separation from name envelopes\n   181\t  (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 6.3). The primitive is `yin.vm.linker.sign`'s\n   182\t  Ed25519, unchanged. The principal is its own key.\n   183\t- **The sequence is derived.** It is the greatest transaction `t` among\n   184\t  the datoms of the index the manifest names: a nonnegative integer, and\n   185\t  **0 is valid**, since the first transaction is `t = 0`\n   186\t  (`src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc:117-131`). An index of no datoms\n   187\t  has no trace. The publisher stores no counter.\n   188\t- **A reader confirms it.** A loaded index whose greatest `t` is not the\n   189\t  trace's sequence refuses the trace, `:yin.head/seq-mismatch`.\n   190\t- **Proven for the REPL path, enforced for every caller.** That two HEAD\n   191\t  writes of one directory never share a greatest `t`, across restart and\n   192\t  `(reset)`, holds for `yin.repl.index` (`index.cljc:189-233, 378-435`)\n   193\t  and H0 pins it. `deposit!` takes the datoms, never a sequence, and\n   194\t  refuses within one process a sequence equal to the last it signed with\n   195\t  another manifest (`:yin.head/seq-collision`) or below it\n   196\t  (`:yin.head/seq-regression`).\n   197\t- **The key kept and the directory lost** restarts the sequence; readers\n   198\t  refuse the new traces as stale. The remedy is\n   199\t  `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 6.5's, unchanged and still available (5.8):\n   200\t  hydrate the directory from the last published manifest before\n   201\t  publishing, or take a new key.\n   202\t\n   203\tRejected: the `m` slot (C1); a stored counter; a `:prev` chain.\n   204\t\n   205\t### 5.3 Retention (decision 3)\n   206\t\n   207\t**Capacity 1. The latest head wins and the ring coalesces.**\n   208\t\n   209\t- **First contact.** The reader mints `:dao.stream/oldest`; on a\n   210\t  capacity-1 ring that is the current head. An empty ring is `blocked`.\n   211\t- **A slow reader** is answered `gap` with the recovery cursor, the\n   212\t  current head's position, and adopts it.\n   213\t- **Anything else is a lost source.** On `cursor-mismatch`, `end`,\n   214\t  `not-found`, or a `transport-error` that is not retryable, the\n   215\t  follower drops the handle and its cursor and emits `:source-lost` with\n   216\t  the outcome. Its owner attaches a fresh handle; the follower mints\n   217\t  `:oldest` on it. A restarted publisher is this case, whatever the new\n   218\t  ring's identity is and however the transport reports it.\n   219\t\n   220\t### 5.4 Publisher behaviour (decision 4)\n   221\t\n   222\t- One trace is deposited at every HEAD move: after the HEAD write, in\n   223\t  the same call, before any network result, as HEAD itself moves\n   224\t  (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 5.5.6). The deposit is a local append.\n   225\t- The publisher does not batch; the ring does.\n   226\t- At startup a node with a key and a recovered HEAD deposits that HEAD's\n   227\t  trace, so a reader's first contact needs no new round.\n   228\t- A node deposits when it has a key, a socket and `:publish?`.\n   229\t- **`announce!` and the ledger ring stay as they are, beside the\n   230\t  board.** The ledger ring is the node's private work queue of put\n   231\t  addresses; `dao.space.dht` holds no key and knows nothing of `yin.*`.\n   232\t  The composition that calls `announce!` calls `deposit!` after it.\n   233\t\n   234\t### 5.5 Reader behaviour (decision 5)\n   235\t\n   236\t**Following** is composition: `--dht-follow <64 hex>@<host:port>`, or\n   237\t`:follow` to the plain API. A followed principal must be declared\n   238\t(`--dht-principal`); otherwise startup is refused.\n   239\t\n   240\t**Per principal the follower keeps three things, each with one job.**\n   241\t\n   242\t<table>\n   243\t<tr><th>Thing</th><th>What it is</th><th>What it decides</th></tr>\n   244\t<tr><td>The <strong>floor</strong></td>\n   245\t<td>The installed head's sequence, or <code>nil</code>: none was ever\n   246\tinstalled. Read from the persisted trace (5.7).</td>\n   247\t<td>Replay and rollback, and nothing else.</td></tr>\n   248\t<tr><td>The <strong>candidate</strong></td>\n   249\t<td>At most one verified trace above the floor. Process state.</td>\n   250\t<td>What is loading. It proves nothing and raises nothing.</td></tr>\n   251\t<tr><td>The <strong>installed head</strong></td>\n   252\t<td>One trace: verified, loaded, confirmed by its index, persisted.</td>\n   253\t<td>The snapshot set and the floor.</td></tr>\n   254\t</table>\n   255\t\n   256\t**The rule for one observed trace** is `judge`, a pure function of the\n   257\tfollowed principal, the floor, the installed manifest and the trace:\n   258\t\n   259\t<table>\n   260\t<tr><th>Case</th><th>Outcome</th></tr>\n   261\t<tr><td>Not the closed shape of 5.2, or a sequence that is not a\n   262\tnonnegative integer</td>\n   263\t<td><code>:yin.head/malformed</code></td></tr>\n   264\t<tr><td>Principal is not the followed one</td>\n   265\t<td><code>:yin.head/wrong-principal</code></td></tr>\n   266\t<tr><td>Signature does not verify</td>\n   267\t<td><code>:yin.head/bad-proof</code></td></tr>\n   268\t<tr><td>The floor is <code>nil</code></td>\n   269\t<td><code>:candidate</code>, whatever the sequence, 0 included</td></tr>\n   270\t<tr><td>Sequence below the floor</td>\n   271\t<td><code>:yin.head/stale</code></td></tr>\n   272\t<tr><td>Sequence at the floor, the installed manifest</td>\n   273\t<td><code>:duplicate</code></td></tr>\n   274\t<tr><td>Sequence at the floor, another manifest</td>\n   275\t<td><code>:yin.head/equivocation</code></td></tr>\n   276\t<tr><td>Sequence above the floor</td>\n   277\t<td><code>:candidate</code></td></tr>\n   278\t</table>\n   279\t\n   280\t- **`judge` has no source argument and no candidate argument.** Its\n   281\t  answer for a trace is the same whoever carried it, in whatever order,\n   282\t  and whatever is loading. One source is the degenerate case of many.\n   283\t- **The floor is confirmed evidence only.** It moves at installation\n   284\t  and at no other time. A candidate's sequence is a claim and is never\n   285\t  compared against a later trace.\n   286\t\n   287\t**The candidate: latest observed wins.**\n   288\t\n   289\t- A trace judged `:candidate` **replaces** the current candidate, unless\n   290\t  it is the same trace or the one last rejected (below). It replaces a\n   291\t  candidate of a higher sequence too: the source's newest element is its\n   292\t  current claim, and one slot has no capacity to exhaust.\n   293\t- **A replaced candidate is detached at once, and the new one starts\n   294\t  loading in the same step.** The follower does not wait for the old\n   295\t  load. If the old load is the follower's own and still `:loading`, it\n   296\t  is **abandoned**: `(dao.space.dht/abandon node address)` removes a\n   297\t  `:loading` record and emits nothing. If it is terminal it is\n   298\t  forgotten. The one fetch it may have outstanding ends by the DHT's own\n   299\t  `get-ticks` and its completion matches no load. So a newer head never\n   300\t  waits on an older one, no deadline is added, and nothing accumulates.\n   301\t- The candidate's load is `dao.space.dht/load` with the covered-index\n   302\t  walk under the kind `:yin.head/candidate`. `loaded-indexes` lists only\n   303\t  index-kind loads (`dao/space/dht.cljc:1214-1227`), so **a candidate is\n   304\t  in no snapshot set and no name resolves through it**, with no change\n   305\t  to that function.\n   306\t- **Kinds do not mix (D3).** `load` of an address already recorded under\n   307\t  another kind is refused, `:dao.space.dht/kind-conflict`, instead of\n   308\t  silently left. If the candidate's manifest is already loaded by hand\n   309\t  (index kind), the follower starts nothing and reads that record; it\n   310\t  never forgets or abandons a record that is not of its own kind. A\n   311\t  `load-index` by hand of a manifest that is loading as a candidate is\n   312\t  told so, and succeeds once the head is installed.\n   313\t- **Confirmed.** The load is `:loaded` and the greatest `t` of its\n   314\t  datoms equals the sequence. The follower emits `:confirmed` with the\n   315\t  trace and changes nothing else (5.7).\n   316\t- **Rejected.** The load is `:loaded` and the check fails:\n   317\t  `:yin.head/seq-mismatch`. The candidate is dropped, its own record\n   318\t  forgotten, and its envelope id kept as the principal's **last\n   319\t  rejected**, one value, so the same trace shown at every poll is not\n   320\t  loaded again. The floor is where it was. Any other candidate trace\n   321\t  takes the slot as usual.\n   322\t- **Unloadable.** The load failed: `:yin.head/unloadable` with the\n   323\t  failure as data. The candidate stays. After a delay in node ticks,\n   324\t  doubling from `:repair-ticks` to `:repair-max-ticks`, the failed\n   325\t  record is forgotten and the load started again **under the kind the\n   326\t  record had**, so a failed load made by hand at that address is\n   327\t  restarted as an index load and stays the user's. No deadline ends the\n   328\t  retries; a newer observed trace replaces the candidate.\n   329\t\n   330\t**The snapshot set** (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 7.2, amended) is: the\n   331\tmanifest the directory's HEAD names; every index-kind load that is\n   332\t`:loaded`; and **the installed head of each followed principal**, read\n   333\tfrom the node's local store, where every blob of it already is. The\n   334\tfollower records the installed manifests on the node value;\n   335\t`yin.vm.linker.dht/snapshots` reads them. Resolution (7.3) is otherwise\n   336\tunchanged.\n   337\t\n   338\t- The reader's own HEAD is never written by following.\n   339\t- Installation replaces the principal's installed manifest in one step.\n   340\t  No fold sees two heads of one principal, or none.\n   341\t\n   342\t**A silent publisher** changes nothing. The follower records the node\n   343\ttick reading at which its source last answered, as data for a drive\n   344\tthat wants to judge liveness with `dao.lease`. Nothing here reads it.\n   345\t\n   346\t### 5.6 When `(require 'alib)` refreshes (decision 6)\n   347\t\n   348\t**On a head event.** A require never asks the network for a newer head.\n   349\t\n   350\t- A `require` that links resolves against the snapshot set as it stands.\n   351\t- **A name may change meaning between two requires**: in two sessions,\n   352\t  or before and after `(reset)`. The require's provenance carries the\n   353\t  snapshot vector.\n   354\t- **Dependency mismatches surface sooner.** When `base` is republished\n   355\t  and a reader follows both `base` and `app`, `app`'s require refuses\n   356\t  `:yin.link.dht/dependency-binding` with `:mismatch` (7.4) until `app`\n   357\t  is republished. Following does not create this; it stops hiding it.\n   358\t- **First contact.** A require whose name is `:absent` while some\n   359\t  followed principal has no installed head parks pending on that\n   360\t  principal (R10) and is re-checked when its head is installed (R11's\n   361\t  shape). `(abandon)` and the link policy end it.\n   362\t- `(yin.head/heads)` answers, per principal, what is installed, the\n   363\t  candidate and its state, and the last refusal.\n   364\t\n   365\t**Pinning.** `--dht-manifest <address>` is unchanged: it hydrates an\n   366\tempty directory from that manifest, one-shot and never saved. A run\n   367\tgiven it follows nothing: with a saved `--dht-follow`, the pin wins for\n   368\tthat run and the banner says following is suspended. A direct entry\n   369\t(`--link-name my.lib=<address>`) still pins one name.\n   370\t\n   371\t### 5.7 Persistence and relink (decision 7)\n   372\t\n   373\t**Persistence.** The follower's durable state is the last installed\n   374\ttrace of each followed principal, verbatim, in `<node dir>/heads.edn`:\n   375\t\n   376\t```clojure\n   377\t{:version 1\n   378\t :heads {\"ed25519:<hex>\" {:yin.head/envelope {...} :yin.head/proof {...}}}}\n   379\t```\n   380\t\n   381\t- The whole signed trace is kept, not a manifest and a number: the floor\n   382\t  and the manifest are read from evidence that verifies again.\n   383\t- **Durable before used.** A confirmed head is installed in this order:\n   384\t  1. The follower emits `:confirmed` with the trace.\n   385\t  2. The owner replaces `heads.edn` atomically with that trace in its\n   386\t     principal's place.\n   387\t  3. Only when the write has returned does the owner call\n   388\t     `(head/install follower node principal trace)`: the head enters the\n   389\t     snapshot set, the floor moves, `:installed` is emitted.\n   390\t- Nothing reads a confirmed head before step 3.\n   391\t- A crash before step 2 restarts at the previous head, which is the last\n   392\t  one ever used. A crash after it restarts at the new head, whose blobs\n   393\t  are local. No crash restarts below a head that was exposed.\n   394\t- **A write that fails installs nothing.** The owner reports\n   395\t  `:yin.head/unpersisted` with the host's reason, the previous head\n   396\t  stays installed, and the write is tried again after the node's repair\n   397\t  delay. A newer confirmed head replaces the waiting one.\n   398\t- A composition with no durable directory declares `:heads :volatile`\n   399\t  and calls `install` on `:confirmed`. Its floor does not survive a\n   400\t  restart, by its own declaration.\n   401\t- At startup each record is verified again, checked against the followed\n   402\t  set, and its index walked in the local store. A record that fails\n   403\t  refuses startup naming it; nothing falls back to no floor.\n   404\t- An index loaded by hand stays process state, as now.\n   405\t\n   406\tRejected: recording observed traces as datoms in the reader's own index\n   407\t(two nodes following each other would move each other's HEAD forever).\n   408\t\n   409\t**Relink: a session keeps what it linked; `(reset)` leads to the new\n   410\tmodule.** `yin.vm` learns nothing, and a repeat `(require 'alib)` of a\n   411\tlinked name is what it is today. When an installed head makes a linked\n   412\tname resolve elsewhere, the shell prints one line: the name, both\n   413\taddresses, the principal and sequence, and that `(reset)` then\n   414\t`(require ...)` links it. The set is derived (the registry against the\n   415\tfold) and stored nowhere; `(yin.head/moved)` answers it.\n   416\t\n   417\t### 5.8 Command line and saved state (decision 8)\n   418\t\n   419\t- **The token** is `yin:<host:port>/<principal>`, printed once when both\n   420\t  the socket and the board endpoint are bound. A token with a third part\n   421\t  is refused: a manifest is a pin and belongs to `--dht-manifest`.\n   422\t- **`dht join <token>`** becomes `--index-store dht:<dir> --dht-peer\n   423\t  <host:port> --dht-principal <hex> --dht-follow <hex>@<host:port>`. It\n   424\t  passes no `--dht-manifest`: its first manifest is the first trace it\n   425\t  installs. The shell admits evaluation at once and says, per followed\n   426\t  principal, whether a head is installed.\n   427\t- **`state.edn`** keeps flags, and gains `--dht-follow` (repeatable).\n   428\t  It never holds a head. `--dht-manifest` stays one-shot.\n   429\t- **Hydration is not removed and `--dht-recover` is not added.** The\n   430\t  defect was joining by hydration (D1), and join no longer does. What\n   431\t  `--dht-manifest` does is still right for its two remaining uses: a\n   432\t  reproducible run in a fresh directory, and a publisher restoring a\n   433\t  lost directory under a kept key, which is `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 6.5's\n   434\t  documented remedy. Leaving it alone keeps that remedy with no\n   435\t  amendment and no new flag.\n   436\t- A directory hydrated in an earlier run and following in a later one\n   437\t  folds both snapshots. That is correct (the fold deduplicates\n   438\t  envelopes and honours retractions) and is stated, not prevented.\n   439\t- If the TCP port of the same number cannot be bound, the node serves\n   440\t  no board, prints no token and names the port; the operator chooses\n   441\t  another `--dht-port`.\n   442\t\n   443\t### 5.9 Failure and security (decision 9)\n   444\t\n   445\t- A trace is honoured only when it verifies under a declared key. Where\n   446\t  it was read is never evidence.\n   447\t- A head is installed only after its index is local, its sequence is\n   448\t  confirmed by that index, and its trace is durable.\n   449\t- The floor never moves down and moves only at installation.\n   450\t- No failure retracts an installed head, and none waits on a clock.\n   451\t- **Nothing above mentions loopback.** Loopback bounds who can connect.\n   452\t  No rule trusts a source for being local (section 8.4).\n   453\t\n   454\tSection 10 is the table.\n   455\t\n   456\t### 5.10 Portability (decision 10)\n   457\t\n   458\tPortable `.cljc`: the trace, `judge`, the follower, the board, the\n   459\trendering of `heads.edn`, and the ws composition (`dao.stream.ws`,\n   460\t`dao.stream.ws-project`, `dao.stream.remote`). Host seams, all existing:\n   461\tEd25519 (`yin.vm.linker.sign`), the WebSocket listener and dialer\n   462\t(`src/clj/dao/stream/ws/jvm.clj`, `src/cljs/dao/stream/ws/node.cljs`,\n   463\t`src/cljd/dao/stream/ws/dart.cljd`), the atomic file replace\n   464\t(`dao.space.store.fs`). A browser can dial but has no Ed25519 here: it\n   465\tfollows nothing yet.\n   466\t\n   467\tClojureDart traps: `heads.edn` is rendered with no whitespace before a\n   468\tclosing bracket; host branches put `:cljd` first; no protocol method\n   469\ttakes `[_ _]`; no test reaches a private var by `#'`; the sequence stays\n   470\twithin 2^53 and the vectors include one above 2^32; a manifest address\n   471\tfrom the wire is compared with `=`; a trace decoded from the channel\n   472\tcodec is verified over `dao.jing/canonical-bytes`, asserted on all\n   473\tthree hosts.\n   474\t\n   475\t## 6. The plain Clojure API\n   476\t\n   477\t```text\n   478\tjoin -> publish! -> assert! -> announce! -> deposit!        (publisher)\n   479\tjoin -> follow -> attach -> step ... -> names -> link       (reader)\n   480\t```\n   481\t\n   482\t<table>\n   483\t<tr><th>Function</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>\n   484\t<tr><td><code>(yin.vm.linker.head/verify trace)</code></td>\n   485\t<td>True or false; never throws.</td></tr>\n   486\t<tr><td><code>(head/seq-of datoms)</code></td>\n   487\t<td>The greatest <code>t</code>; <code>nil</code> for no datoms.</td></tr>\n   488\t<tr><td><code>(head/judge principal floor installed trace)</code></td>\n   489\t<td>The rule of 5.5, as data.</td></tr>\n   490\t<tr><td><code>(head/board)</code>,\n   491\t<code>(head/deposit! board key manifest datoms)</code></td>\n   492\t<td>The capacity-1 ring, and sign-and-append; refuses\n   493\t<code>:yin.head/seq-collision</code>, <code>:seq-regression</code>,\n   494\t<code>:empty</code>, <code>:no-key</code>.</td></tr>\n   495\t<tr><td><code>(head/follow node opts)</code></td>\n   496\t<td>A follower: <code>:follow</code> principals, <code>:heads</code> the\n   497\tpersisted records or <code>:volatile</code>.</td></tr>\n   498\t<tr><td><code>(head/attach follower principal reader)</code></td>\n   499\t<td>Give the follower a <code>dao.stream</code> reader handle for that\n   500\tprincipal's board. Any handle.</td></tr>\n   501\t<tr><td><code>(head/step follower node now)</code></td>\n   502\t<td>One pass after <code>dao.space.dht/step</code>:\n   503\t<code>[follower node events]</code>. It confirms; it never installs.\n   504\t</td></tr>\n   505\t<tr><td><code>(head/install follower node principal trace)</code></td>\n   506\t<td>Step 3 of 5.7. Refused unless that trace is the confirmed one.</td>\n   507\t</tr>\n   508\t<tr><td><code>(head/heads follower)</code>,\n   509\t<code>(head/records follower principal trace)</code></td>\n   510\t<td>The status of 5.6, and the value <code>heads.edn</code> holds once\n   511\tthat trace is written.</td></tr>\n   512\t<tr><td><code>(head/moved names registry)</code></td>\n   513\t<td>The linked names whose resolved address differs.</td></tr>\n   514\t<tr><td><code>yin.vm.linker.head.ws/serve</code>, <code>serve-step</code>,\n   515\t<code>dial</code>, <code>dial-step</code></td>\n   516\t<td>The board's acceptor and the reader's dial, thin compositions of\n   517\t<code>dao.stream.ws-project</code>; the listener and the attacher are\n   518\targuments. The only namespace here that knows a transport.</td></tr>\n   519\t</table>\n   520\t\n   521\t`dao.space.dht` gains `abandon` and the kind-conflict refusal, and\n   522\tlearns nothing about heads. Events are maps under `:yin.head/event`:\n   523\t`:confirmed`, `:installed`, `:refused` (a reason and the trace),\n   524\t`:unloadable`, `:source-lost`. `yin.repl` adds a `yin.head` host module,\n   525\t`heads` and `moved`, each one call into the functions above; the shell\n   526\towns the file, the lines, the flags, and the dial.\n   527\t\n   528\t## 7. Limits and defaults\n   529\t\n   530\t<table>\n   531\t<tr><th>Datum</th><th>Default</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>\n   532\t<tr><td>Board capacity</td><td>1</td><td>Fixed.</td></tr>\n   533\t<tr><td>Candidates per principal</td><td>1</td><td>Fixed (5.5).</td></tr>\n   534\t<tr><td><code>:head-poll-ticks</code></td><td>5000</td>\n   535\t<td>Node ticks between two <code>next</code> calls on one source. It\n   536\tbounds how often a reader asks, not how late it learns.</td></tr>\n   537\t<tr><td>Retry delays (load, write, re-dial)</td>\n   538\t<td><code>:repair-ticks</code> to <code>:repair-max-ticks</code></td>\n   539\t<td>Doubling, the node's existing bounds.</td></tr>\n   540\t<tr><td>Followed principals</td><td>at most 64</td>\n   541\t<td>More refuses startup.</td></tr>\n   542\t<tr><td>Board endpoint slots</td><td>8</td>\n   543\t<td>The acceptor's handoff slots, as the REPL endpoint's.</td></tr>\n   544\t</table>\n   545\t\n   546\tFollowing never makes the node `busy?`.\n   547\t\n   548\t## 8. Cross-machine: the owner's question\n   549\t\n   550\t> if the abstraction is dao.stream, cross-machine would not require a\n   551\t> complete redesign, right?\n   552\t\n   553\t**Right.** The core is built so that it is true, and the one place it\n   554\twould have been false is removed. Following across machines changes how\n   555\ta reader handle is obtained and who may connect to the board. It changes\n   556\tno trace, no rule, no file and no reader semantics.\n   557\t\n   558\t### 8.1 What is transport-agnostic, and stays so\n   559\t\n   560\t<table>\n   561\t<tr><th>Part</th><th>Why it cannot depend on a transport</th></tr>\n   562\t<tr><td>The trace and its signature (5.2)</td>\n   563\t<td>No address, no time, no channel datum is signed.</td></tr>\n   564\t<tr><td><code>judge</code> (5.5)</td>\n   565\t<td>No source argument. One source is the degenerate case.</td></tr>\n   566\t<tr><td>Confirmation (5.2, 5.5)</td>\n   567\t<td>The index is content-addressed and fetched by the DHT, which\n   568\tverifies every blob against its address.</td></tr>\n   569\t<tr><td>Persistence (5.7)</td>\n   570\t<td><code>heads.edn</code> holds signed traces and no source.</td></tr>\n   571\t<tr><td>The follower (5.3, 5.5)</td>\n   572\t<td>It holds a reader handle and calls <code>cursor</code> and\n   573\t<code>next</code>. A lost source is one event, whatever lost it.</td>\n   574\t</tr>\n   575\t<tr><td>Reader semantics (5.6, 5.7)</td>\n   576\t<td>They start at the snapshot set.</td></tr>\n   577\t</table>\n   578\t\n   579\tOnly `yin.vm.linker.head.ws` and the shell's flags know a transport.\n   580\tSlice H1 proves the claim by test: the follower is driven to completion\n   581\tover a plain local ring, with no channel at all.\n   582\t\n   583\t### 8.2 What each later step adds and amends\n   584\t\n   585\t<table>\n   586\t<tr><th>Step</th><th>Adds</th><th>Amends</th>\n   587\t<th>Changes the core's trace, rule or persistence?</th></tr>\n   588\t<tr><td><strong>Serving off loopback</strong> (8.3)</td>\n   589\t<td>A bind that is not loopback; a bound on concurrent sessions; a\n   590\tbanner line.</td>\n   591\t<td>This document 5.1 and 5.8; <code>yin.vm.linker.head.ws</code>;\n   592\t<code>yin.repl.main</code>. Nothing in <code>dao.stream.*</code> if the\n   593\tunverified items of 8.3 hold.</td>\n   594\t<td><strong>No.</strong></td></tr>\n   595\t<tr><td><strong>Relaying</strong></td>\n   596\t<td>A second ring per followed principal holding the installed trace\n   597\tverbatim, read from <code>heads.edn</code>; a flag.</td>\n   598\t<td><code>dao.stream.remote.md</code> 2 and 5: C3 must be settled,\n   599\tbecause one name then reaches many rings (bless service names, or a\n   600\tnamed <code>descriptor</code> request that answers the ring's own\n   601\tdescriptor). <code>dao.stream.remote</code> if the second.</td>\n   602\t<td><strong>No.</strong> It is why the trace carries no address and\n   603\twhy <code>heads.edn</code> keeps the whole signed trace.</td></tr>\n   604\t<tr><td><strong>Many sources per principal</strong></td>\n   605\t<td>A cursor per source; a scheduling policy for which candidate loads\n   606\twhen sources disagree.</td>\n   607\t<td>This document 5.5 \"The candidate\" and section 7; the follower's\n   608\tprocess state and the shape <code>heads</code> answers.</td>\n   609\t<td><strong>No</strong> for the trace, <code>judge</code>, the floor\n   610\tand <code>heads.edn</code>. <strong>Yes for one plumbing rule:</strong>\n   611\t\"latest observed wins\" is sound only for one writer's ring. Many\n   612\tsources need \"greatest verified sequence, with a fair turn for an\n   613\tunloadable one\", which is where the two scheduling defects of the\n   614\tsecond review live. That design starts from those findings.</td></tr>\n   615\t<tr><td><strong>UDP on the DHT socket</strong></td>\n   616\t<td>A mirror on the node's datagram socket; a return-path proof.</td>\n   617\t<td><code>dao.jing.dht.md</code> 3 and 8 (frozen);\n   618\t<code>dao.stream.remote.md</code> 3.2; <code>dao.stream.udp</code>:\n   619\tthe proof must ride the outer fragment envelope and be checked before\n   620\treassembly, because a fragment carries no request key\n   621\t(<code>src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:135-149, 312-325</code>);\n   622\t<code>dao.space.dht</code>.</td>\n   623\t<td><strong>No.</strong> It is the costliest step and touches the core\n   624\tnowhere.</td></tr>\n   625\t</table>\n   626\t\n   627\t**The redesign risks, found now.** Three, none in the core:\n   628\t\n   629\t1. C3 is unsettled in the contract and already depended on by\n   630\t   `yin.repl`. It blocks relaying, not cross-machine following.\n   631\t2. The single-candidate rule does not generalise to many sources. It is\n   632\t   one paragraph of 5.5, and it is labelled as such.\n   633\t3. The token assumes TCP and UDP share one external port number. Behind\n   634\t   a NAT that maps them apart it needs a second number. A third token\n   635\t   part of the form `ws:<port>` is reserved for that; it is additive.\n   636\t\n   637\t### 8.3 The first cross-machine transport: WebSocket\n   638\t\n   639\t<table>\n   640\t<tr><th></th><th><code>dao.stream.ws</code></th>\n   641\t<th>UDP on the DHT socket, plus a return-path proof</th></tr>\n   642\t<tr><td>Reflection of traffic at a spoofed source</td>\n   643\t<td>None: a WebSocket rides TCP, whose handshake is the return-path\n   644\tproof.</td>\n   645\t<td>The problem itself. Needs a new proof, on fragments too.</td></tr>\n   646\t<tr><td>Serving an arbitrary ring with existing code</td>\n   647\t<td><strong>Yes.</strong> <code>ws-project/make-acceptor</code> takes\n   648\tany mirror table and <code>accept-step!</code> runs\n   649\t<code>remote/mirror-step</code> per accepted connection\n   650\t(<code>ws_project.cljc:161-190, 248-262</code>);\n   651\t<code>dial</code>, <code>dial-attach!</code> and\n   652\t<code>dial-step!</code> give the reader a reflection\n   653\t(<code>305-420</code>). <code>yin.repl.serve</code> does exactly this\n   654\ttoday with a two-entry table (<code>serve.cljc:224-225, 291</code>).\n   655\t</td>\n   656\t<td>No. The node has no mirror on its socket; that is new code in\n   657\t<code>dao.space.dht</code>.</td></tr>\n   658\t<tr><td>Contracts amended</td><td>None expected.</td>\n   659\t<td>Two, one of them frozen.</td></tr>\n   660\t<tr><td>NAT</td>\n   661\t<td>One side must accept a TCP connection: a public address or a\n   662\tforwarded port. No hole punching. Either side may be the one that\n   663\tdials, so no node is privileged, but two peers that are both behind\n   664\tNATs need a relay pair\n   665\t(<code>dao.stream.remote.md</code> 3.3, 4).</td>\n   666\t<td>Hole punching is possible in principle, through a meeting peer.\n   667\tThe DHT has none today (<code>dao.jing.dht.md</code> section 6: \"NAT\n   668\tmeeting is not in this epic\").</td></tr>\n   669\t<tr><td>What the reader must already reach</td>\n   670\t<td colspan=\"2\">The publisher's UDP socket, to fetch blobs. With no NAT\n   671\tmeeting in the DHT, cross-machine use today already needs the publisher\n   672\tdirectly reachable. WebSocket asks for the same host on TCP as\n   673\twell.</td></tr>\n   674\t<tr><td>Token</td>\n   675\t<td><code>yin:&lt;host:port&gt;/&lt;principal&gt;</code>, the port\n   676\tnumber shared (5.1); <code>ws:&lt;port&gt;</code> reserved.</td>\n   677\t<td>The same two parts, one socket.</td></tr>\n   678\t<tr><td>Confidentiality</td>\n   679\t<td>None: <code>wss://</code> \"has no settled descriptor form\"\n   680\t(<code>src/cljc/yin/repl/connect.cljc:259</code>). The head is public\n   681\tand its integrity is the signature's.</td>\n   682\t<td>None either.</td></tr>\n   683\t</table>\n   684\t\n   685\t**Recommendation: WebSocket, for the core and for the first step off\n   686\tloopback.** It is the transport with a return path, it serves the ring\n   687\twith code that exists, and it amends no contract. UDP on the DHT socket\n   688\tstays the deferred alternative, wanted when hole-punched peers must\n   689\tfollow each other without a relay.\n   690\t\n   691\t**What that step is.** Remove the loopback condition of 5.1, and land\n   692\tthe checks below. No design beyond this section is expected.\n   693\t\n   694\t**Unverified, and to be verified by that step before it lands:**\n   695\t\n   696\t- That the acceptor bounds concurrent sessions. The handoff has 8\n   697\t  slots; whether `:sessions` is bounded under many held connections was\n   698\t  not checked.\n   699\t- That one connection cannot make a step long: `mirror-step` loops to\n   700\t  `blocked`, and the spec's \"composition budget\" was not found in it.\n   701\t- That the listener seams, written for `yin.repl.host`, compose outside\n   702\t  `yin.repl.serve` on all three hosts without moving code.\n   703\t- That a TCP listener binds at the UDP socket's number on each host,\n   704\t  including after an ephemeral UDP bind.\n   705\t\n   706\t### 8.4 The assumption cross-machine adds: locality is not trust\n   707\t\n   708\tOn loopback, whoever connects is on the machine. Off it, anyone is.\n   709\tNothing in the core relied on the first:\n   710\t\n   711\t- **Reading the board** was never restricted. A head is public wherever\n   712\t  its node is reachable, as its index is under `--dht-publish`.\n   713\t- **A hostile or intercepted source** can do three things: show a\n   714\t  forged trace (`:bad-proof`), show an old one (`:stale` against a\n   715\t  durable floor), or show nothing. `judge` already treats every source\n   716\t  that way; it has no notion of a local one.\n   717\t- **First contact has no floor**, on loopback or off. Off loopback an\n   718\t  interceptor on a cleartext channel can show a first-time reader an\n   719\t  old valid head, or withhold. That limit was always in section 10; the\n   720\t  network makes it reachable. `wss` or a second source narrows it;\n   721\t  neither is in the core.\n   722\t- **The index** is fetched by content address and confirmed against the\n   723\t  trace.\n   724\t- **What does change** is resource exposure: connections, sessions,\n   725\t  step time. Those are the unverified items of 8.3, and they are the\n   726\t  whole of the work.\n   727\t\n   728\t## 9. What is rejected\n   729\t\n   730\t- **A head under a DHT key.** C2; it is the mutable-pointer service.\n   731\t- **The signature in `m`.** C1.\n   732\t- **UDP on the DHT socket first.** 8.3.\n   733\t- **A candidate set.** With one source it buys nothing and its capacity\n   734\t  is a way to starve a true head.\n   735\t- **A floor that includes unconfirmed claims.** A replayed, validly\n   736\t  signed, wrong trace would shut out every true head below it.\n   737\t- **Install, then persist.** A reader that used head 20 could restart\n   738\t  at 19.\n   739\t- **Load holders in `dao.space.dht`** (revision 1). A distinct load\n   740\t  kind keeps candidates out of the snapshot set with no new bookkeeping,\n   741\t  and `forget` keeps its contract.\n   742\t- **Removing hydration, and `--dht-recover`** (revision 1). 5.8.\n   743\t- **The board on the REPL endpoint's table.** It would publish the\n   744\t  evaluation service with it.\n   745\t- **A head operation on `dao.stream.apply`, a DHT wire op, a hash\n   746\t  chain, push.** As before: each adds a mechanism where a ring served as\n   747\t  itself needs none.\n   748\t\n   749\t## 10. Failure modes\n   750\t\n   751\t<table>\n   752\t<tr><th>Event</th><th>What the reader does</th><th>Limit</th></tr>\n   753\t<tr><td>A forged trace</td>\n   754\t<td><code>:yin.head/bad-proof</code>.</td><td>-</td></tr>\n   755\t<tr><td>An old trace shown again</td>\n   756\t<td><code>:yin.head/stale</code> against the durable floor.</td>\n   757\t<td><strong>First contact has no floor</strong>: an old valid head is\n   758\tinstalled. No trace says how new it is.</td></tr>\n   759\t<tr><td>A signed trace whose sequence is wrong</td>\n   760\t<td>Loaded, <code>:yin.head/seq-mismatch</code>, dropped, remembered.\n   761\tThe floor does not move; its index is never resolvable.</td>\n   762\t<td>It is loaded once per process.</td></tr>\n   763\t<tr><td>A publisher that rewinds</td>\n   764\t<td>A lower <code>t</code> is <code>:stale</code>.</td>\n   765\t<td>A key holder can sign a new index that omits anything. Declaring a\n   766\tprincipal is trusting it for its own head.</td></tr>\n   767\t<tr><td>Two manifests at one sequence</td>\n   768\t<td><code>:yin.head/equivocation</code>; the installed head stays.</td>\n   769\t<td>A greater sequence ends it.</td></tr>\n   770\t<tr><td>The source shows nothing</td>\n   771\t<td>Nothing. The installed head stays.</td>\n   772\t<td><strong>Withholding is undetectable</strong>: silence and \"no new\n   773\thead\" look the same, and no timeout may tell them apart.</td></tr>\n   774\t<tr><td>A manifest nobody holds</td>\n   775\t<td><code>:yin.head/unloadable</code>; retried at the bounded delay;\n   776\tthe next observed trace replaces it at once.</td><td>-</td></tr>\n   777\t<tr><td>An older load never finishes</td>\n   778\t<td>Abandoned the moment a newer trace is observed; the newer one\n   779\tloads at once (5.5).</td><td>-</td></tr>\n   780\t<tr><td>A crash while installing</td>\n   781\t<td>Before the write: the previous head. After: the new one.</td>\n   782\t<td>-</td></tr>\n   783\t<tr><td><code>heads.edn</code> cannot be written</td>\n   784\t<td><code>:yin.head/unpersisted</code>; nothing installed; retried.</td>\n   785\t<td>No progress while the directory refuses writes.</td></tr>\n   786\t<tr><td>The publisher restarts, or the connection drops</td>\n   787\t<td><code>:source-lost</code>; a fresh handle; <code>:oldest</code>\n   788\tagain.</td><td>-</td></tr>\n   789\t<tr><td>The publisher's directory is lost, the key kept</td>\n   790\t<td>Traces from an empty directory are <code>:stale</code>.</td>\n   791\t<td>The publisher's remedy is 6.5's (5.2).</td></tr>\n   792\t<tr><td>The key is disclosed</td>\n   793\t<td>Whoever holds it moves the head. Remove the declaration.</td>\n   794\t<td>-</td></tr>\n   795\t</table>\n   796\t\n   797\t## 11. Implementation slices\n   798\t\n   799\tOrder: **H0 -> H1 -> H2 -> H3.** Each lands alone, green on the JVM,\n   800\tNode and Dart. H0 and H1 own disjoint source files.\n   801\t\n   802\t**H0 -- the trace and its rule.** Pure. Files: new\n   803\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head.cljc` (`trace`, `verify`, `seq-of`,\n   804\t`judge`), `src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/sign.cljc` (the head prefix),\n   805\t`test/yin/vm/linker/sign_vectors.edn`, new\n   806\t`test/yin/vm/linker/head_test.cljc`. Complete when:\n   807\t- One trace under RFC 8032 TEST 1's seed is in the vectors with its\n   808\t  canonical bytes, signed message and signature, byte for byte on all\n   809\t  three hosts; one has a sequence above 2^32.\n   810\t- Tampering with the principal, manifest or sequence, a flipped\n   811\t  signature bit, another key, an extra key in either map, and a\n   812\t  name-envelope signature offered as a head proof each fail.\n   813\t- A trace through `dao.stream.cbor` verifies on each host.\n   814\t- **Sequence zero.** With a `nil` floor a trace of sequence 0 is a\n   815\t  candidate. With floor 0: sequence 1 a candidate, sequence 0 with the\n   816\t  installed manifest a duplicate, with another `:equivocation`. A\n   817\t  negative or non-integer sequence is `:malformed`. `seq-of` of one\n   818\t  transaction is 0, asserted against `dao.space.transactor`.\n   819\t- `judge` produces every row of 5.5, and its arity admits no source and\n   820\t  no candidate.\n   821\t- `seq-of` over a rehydrated index equals `seq-of` before the restart;\n   822\t  a further round raises it; two HEAD writes of one directory never\n   823\t  share it across rounds, name transactions, `(reset)` and the\n   824\t  unwritten-row recovery of `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 5.1. **If a HEAD\n   825\t  move that commits no transaction is found, the slice stops and 5.2 is\n   826\t  reopened.**\n   827\t\n   828\t**H1 -- the follower, over any reader.** No transport. Files:\n   829\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head.cljc` (`board`, `deposit!`, `follow`,\n   830\t`attach`, `step`, `install`, `heads`, `records`, `moved`),\n   831\t`src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc` (`abandon`; the kind-conflict refusal),\n   832\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/dht.cljc` (`snapshots` adds installed heads),\n   833\t`test/dao/space/dht_test.cljc`, new\n   834\t`test/yin/vm/linker/head_follow_test.cljc`. Amendments carried:\n   835\t`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 1, 7.2, 10 and 13 (D2; `abandon`; the refusal);\n   836\t`dao.jing.dht.md` section 1 (a pointer here). In every case the reader\n   837\tis handed the publisher's board ring directly as its reader handle, and\n   838\tblobs travel over the mesh seam. Complete when:\n   839\t- **Existing tests.** `forget` is untouched, and\n   840\t  `forget-clears-a-terminal-record-and-is-refused-while-loading` passes\n   841\t  as written. Any existing test that loads one address under two kinds\n   842\t  and relies on the silent no-op is migrated, and the slice names it.\n   843\t- `abandon` removes a `:loading` record, emits no event, and is refused\n   844\t  for a record that is not loading. After it, a completion for the\n   845\t  abandoned fetch matches no load, and the node's pending fetch state is\n   846\t  empty once the DHT's `get-ticks` have passed.\n   847\t- A publishes `alib` and deposits; B, given the board and A's principal\n   848\t  and no manifest, installs A's head and resolves `alib`. A republishes;\n   849\t  B resolves the new address.\n   850\t- **First transaction.** A first index deposits sequence 0; a\n   851\t  first-contact reader installs it; sequence 1 replaces it.\n   852\t- Ten deposits between two polls install one head, the last.\n   853\t- **A candidate is not resolvable.** While the candidate is `:loaded`\n   854\t  and not installed, and between `:confirmed` and `install`,\n   855\t  `snapshots` does not list it and a name asserted only in it is\n   856\t  `:absent`.\n   857\t- **A wrong-sequence trace X** (sequence 1000000 over an index at 10,\n   858\t  holding a newer assertion of `alib`), from floor 10 and from a `nil`\n   859\t  floor, with true heads 11 and 12, in each order: X, 11, 12; 11, X, 12;\n   860\t  11, 12, X; X while 11 is loading. Each run ends with the last true\n   861\t  head shown installed, the floor equal to its sequence, `alib` never\n   862\t  resolved through X, and X not loaded a second time when shown again.\n   863\t- **An unfinished load does not block.** The mesh never answers for a\n   864\t  blob of head 11, so its load stays `:loading` for the whole test.\n   865\t  Head 12, whose blobs are all local to the mesh, is deposited: 12 is\n   866\t  installed, 11's record is gone in the step 12 was observed, and no\n   867\t  tick count is asserted to have ended 11.\n   868\t- **An unloadable candidate** keeps the previous head, is started again\n   869\t  only after the delay, and is replaced at once by the next trace.\n   870\t- **A failed load made by hand.** The test calls `load-index` on a\n   871\t  manifest no peer holds; it fails. The publisher's head names that\n   872\t  manifest and the blobs then appear. The follower restarts the load as\n   873\t  an index load after the delay; it loads; the head is installed; the\n   874\t  record is still of the index kind and still listed by\n   875\t  `loaded-indexes`. A `load-index` by hand while a candidate of that\n   876\t  address is loading is `:dao.space.dht/kind-conflict`.\n   877\t- **Durable before used.** After `:confirmed` and before `install`: the\n   878\t  floor, the snapshot set and `heads` under `:installed` are unchanged.\n   879\t  A follower composed again from the old records is at the old head. One\n   880\t  composed again from the new records, `install` never called, is at\n   881\t  the new head with zero `:jing/get` on the request ring, and refuses\n   882\t  the older trace as `:stale`. `install` of any other trace is refused.\n   883\t- **A lost source.** A reader handle answering `cursor-mismatch`, and\n   884\t  one answering `end`, each produce `:source-lost` once; after `attach`\n   885\t  of a fresh handle the current head is read again and judged a\n   886\t  duplicate.\n   887\t- `deposit!` refuses `:seq-collision`, `:seq-regression`, `:empty` and\n   888\t  `:no-key`, each appending nothing.\n   889\t- Two nodes following each other reach quiescence; neither HEAD moves.\n   890\t- `moved` names exactly the linked names whose address changed.\n   891\t\n   892\t**H2 -- the board over WebSocket, on loopback.** Files: new\n   893\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head/ws.cljc`, new\n   894\t`test/yin/vm/linker/head_ws_test.cljc`. Amendment A1 carried:\n   895\t`dao.stream.remote.md` section 5 records the head board beside the\n   896\tREPL service as a convention, and states the ruling on C3 (a stable\n   897\tservice name as a table key: allowed and how a reader must treat the\n   898\tidentity it reports, or disallowed with the replacement). If A1's\n   899\truling is \"disallowed\", this slice stops and 5.1's naming is reopened;\n   900\tnothing else in the core depends on it. Also: the status lines of\n   901\t`dao.stream.remote.md` and `dao.stream.ws.md`, which say \"design\n   902\ttarget\" of code that exists. Complete when:\n   903\t- A serving node and a dialing node on loopback: the reader's handle is\n   904\t  a reflection; H1's first case passes over it unchanged.\n   905\t- The publisher's endpoint is stopped and started: the reader reports\n   906\t  `:source-lost`, dials again, and reads the head, judged a duplicate.\n   907\t- The table holds the board and nothing else; a writer op on it is\n   908\t  `no-surface`; another identity is `not-found`.\n   909\t- A bind host that is not a loopback literal composes no endpoint, for\n   910\t  IPv4 and IPv6 literals.\n   911\t- A TCP port already in use is a refusal as data, and the node keeps\n   912\t  running without a board.\n   913\t- The same cases pass on Node and on Dart.\n   914\t\n   915\t**H3 -- the REPL.** Files: `src/cljc/yin/repl/dht.cljc`,\n   916\t`src/cljc/yin/repl/main.cljc`, `src/cljc/yin/repl/state.cljc`,\n   917\t`src/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc` (the `yin.head` host module),\n   918\t`src/cljc/yin/repl/link.cljc`, `src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc` (the\n   919\tfirst-contact pending), `test/yin/repl/dht_process_test.clj`, the tests\n   920\tbeside each, and the `yin.repl` documents. Complete when:\n   921\t- The token prints once, as `yin:<host:port>/<principal>`, only when\n   922\t  the board endpoint is bound; `parse-token` refuses a third part with\n   923\t  the pin message.\n   924\t- `dht join <token>` passes no `--dht-manifest` and saves\n   925\t  `--dht-follow`; a bare restart resolves from `heads.edn` before any\n   926\t  connection is made.\n   927\t- `--dht-follow` of an undeclared principal refuses startup.\n   928\t  `--dht-manifest` with a saved follow suspends following for that run.\n   929\t- A `(require 'alib)` typed before the first head parks, and completes\n   930\t  on the install with no typed line; `(abandon)` ends it.\n   931\t- **The write precedes the install.** A seam that fails the write\n   932\t  prints `:yin.head/unpersisted`, installs nothing, and installs after\n   933\t  the retry. A process killed between write and install restarts at the\n   934\t  new head; one killed before the write restarts at the old.\n   935\t- An installed head that moves a linked name prints the line of 5.7;\n   936\t  the repeat require is unchanged; `(reset)` then `require` links the\n   937\t  new address.\n   938\t- `heads.edn` cases: absent, malformed, a record that fails\n   939\t  verification, a record for an unfollowed principal; each read on Dart.\n   940\t- **End to end, both processes on loopback.** A publishes `alib`; B\n   941\t  joins with the token and requires it on each of the four VMs, on the\n   942\t  JVM and on Node. A republishes; B, after `(reset)`, links the new\n   943\t  module. B restarted with A stopped still requires it.\n   944\t- The host module holds no rule: no fold, verification or file write in\n   945\t  `yin.repl.query`.\n   946\t\n   947\t## 12. Deferred\n   948\t\n   949\tEach of the first four is its own later design; section 8.2 says what\n   950\tit adds and amends.\n   951\t\n   952\t- **Serving off loopback**, over WebSocket (8.3). First after the core.\n   953\t- **Relaying.** Decided as a deferral: no node re-serves a head in the\n   954\t  core.\n   955\t- **Many sources per principal.**\n   956\t- **UDP on the DHT socket, with a return-path proof**, fragments\n   957\t  included.\n   958\t- **`wss`**, and with it a narrower first-contact limit.\n   959\t- **Finding sources** beyond the address given (rendezvous).\n   960\t- **Liveness** by a `dao.lease` composition over the last-answer reading.\n   961\t- **Relink in place.**\n   962\t- **Resolving a transitive require by its pinned address**\n   963\t  (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` section 13); following makes its absence felt.\n   964\t- **Collecting superseded indexes; key rotation; a head history; a\n   965\t  durable last-rejected; a browser follower; push.**\n   966\t\n   967\t## 13. Questions only the owner can answer\n   968\t\n   969\t1. **Does \"always\" reach into a live session?** The design keeps a\n   970\t   session on the module it linked, reports the move, and links the new\n   971\t   one after `(reset)`. If a repeat `(require 'alib)` must switch a\n   972\t   running session, relink in place needs its own design first. No\n   973\t   ruling settles this.\n   974\t2. **What should a bare `q` see after `dht join`?** Already ruled: the\n   975\t   publisher's facts are a separate db-value and union is explicit.\n   976\t   Open: the default. Today join hydrates, so a bare `q` answers the\n   977\t   publisher's facts. Here join does not, so it answers the reader's own\n   978\t   index, and the publisher's is `(dao.space.dht/q <manifest> ...)` with\n   979\t   the manifest from `(yin.head/heads)`. Choose: (a) that; (b) a bare\n   980\t   `q` unions the installed heads by default; (c) a named view.\n   981\t   Recommendation: (a) now, (c) on request.\n   982\t3. **Is every HEAD move the head you mean?** A follower loads a new\n   983\t   index for each round the publisher evaluates, not only for each\n   984\t   `yin.link/publish`. \"Whatever the HEAD is\" reads that way and the\n   985\t   design follows it; it was said before this cost was stated. The\n   986\t   alternative, a head only at each publish, is quieter and leaves a\n   987\t   follower's view of the publisher's facts behind its names.\n   988\t   Recommendation: every HEAD move.\n   989\t4. **Is WebSocket acceptable as the first cross-machine transport?** It\n   990\t   needs one side to accept a TCP connection, at the same port number\n   991\t   as its UDP socket, and it does not hole-punch: two peers both behind\n   992\t   NATs would need a relay pair, or the deferred UDP design. It is\n   993\t   cleartext until `wss`. It is also the step that needs no contract\n   994\t   amendment and no new proof (8.3). Recommendation: yes.\n   995\t\n   996\tNo longer questions: whether a node relays by default, and the order of\n   997\tthe return-path proof, are deferred with their designs; publisher\n   998\trecovery needs no decision, since its remedy is untouched.\n   999\t\n  1000\t## Revision 1 (2026-10-05), in brief\n  1001\t\n  1002\tThe first review's eight findings were all accepted. What survives of\n  1003\tthat revision here: the `nil` floor and sequence 0; a snapshot set that\n  1004\tnever holds a candidate; a floor of confirmed evidence only; confirm,\n  1005\tpersist, install; loopback-only serving; `deposit!` taking datoms; the\n  1006\tpublisher's recovery remedy kept. What it added and this revision\n  1007\tremoves: load holders, a candidate set, the named `descriptor` request,\n  1008\trelay rings, `--dht-recover`, the removal of hydration, and slice H5.\n  1009\t\n  1010\t## Revision 2 (2026-10-05)\n  1011\t\n  1012\tThe owner chose the simple core and asked whether cross-machine use\n  1013\twould then need a redesign. The document was rewritten, not patched,\n  1014\tand section 8 is the answer. The second review's five findings\n  1015\t(<code>collab/1791205158504-reviewer-architect-head-trace-r2.gpt-6.1-sol<!--\n  1016\t-->.findings.md</code>):\n  1017\t\n  1018\t1. **Candidate eviction starvation (P1): eliminated.** There is one\n  1019\t   candidate slot and no eviction. The latest observed trace takes it,\n  1020\t   even over a higher claimed sequence, so four unloadable high traces\n  1021\t   cannot shut out a true head (5.5).\n  1022\t2. **An unfinished load blocking newer heads (P1): still applicable,\n  1023\t   fixed.** A replaced candidate's load is abandoned in the step the\n  1024\t   newer trace is observed and the newer load starts at once;\n  1025\t   `abandon` is a new function, no deadline is added, and the one\n  1026\t   outstanding fetch ends by the DHT's own bound (5.5; H1's\n  1027\t   \"unfinished load\" case).\n  1028\t3. **Retry of a failed shared load (P2): still applicable in a smaller\n  1029\t   form, fixed.** Kinds no longer mix, and a failed record at the\n  1030\t   candidate's address is forgotten and started again under the kind it\n  1031\t   had (5.5; H1's \"failed load made by hand\" case).\n  1032\t4. **H1's \"existing tests pass unchanged\" (P2): eliminated.** `forget`\n  1033\t   keeps its refusal while loading; the cited test passes as written;\n  1034\t   H1 names any test the kind-conflict refusal migrates.\n  1035\t5. **The cookie cannot gate fragments (P2): eliminated from the core,\n  1036\t   recorded for the deferred design.** No UDP mirror is built. Section\n  1037\t   8.2 makes pre-reassembly proof on the outer fragment envelope a\n  1038\t   stated requirement of that later design, with `dao.stream.udp` and\n  1039\t   `dao.stream.remote.md` in its scope.\n  1040\t\n  1041\tThe first review's finding 4, left \"partly\" resolved: re-checked against\n  1042\tthe one-slot rule and resolved. A wrong or unloadable signed trace can\n  1043\toccupy the slot only while it is the newest thing the source shows; the\n  1044\tnext trace replaces it, and the floor never moved. A source that keeps\n  1045\tshowing it is withholding, which no rule can beat (section 10).\n  1046\t\n  1047\tFound while rewriting:\n  1048\t\n  1049\t- **N5. The tree already uses well-known names as mirror-table\n  1050\t  identities** (`yin.repl/requests`, `yin.repl/answers`). The first\n  1051\t  review's finding 3 was argued from the contract alone; the landed\n  1052\t  REPL service has the same shape. C3 now says so, and amendment A1\n  1053\t  settles it for both instead of for the board alone.\n  1054\t- **N6. The REPL's `--port` endpoint cannot carry the board.** Its table\n  1055\t  holds the evaluation service; exposing the head there would expose\n  1056\t  evaluation with it. The board has its own endpoint (5.1).\n  1057\t- **N7. `dao.space.dht/load` is silent across kinds** (D3).\n  1058\t- **N8. Revision 1 removed hydration to fix D1 and then had to add\n  1059\t  `--dht-recover` to restore what that broke.** The defect was only\n  1060\t  that join used it. Revision 2 stops join using it and touches nothing\n  1061\t  else (5.8).\n  1062\t\n  1063\t## Lineage\n  1064\t\n  1065\tThe tuple space made stigmergy precise; this makes one trace of it\n  1066\tdurable enough to follow. `announce!` was already the deposit, kept\n  1067\tprivate. A head board is the same act with an author and an audience: a\n  1068\tsigned value on a ring one agent writes and any agent may read, carried\n  1069\tby whatever carries a stream. Datomic's connection hands a peer the\n  1070\tnewest database value; here no connection is the abstraction, only the\n  1071\ttrace, and each reader decides whose traces are worth following.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md | sed -n '330,730p'; rg -n 'make-acceptor|accept-step|mirror-step|dial\\\\*|defn dial' src/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc src/cljc/yin/repl/serve.cljc src/cljc/yin/repl/connect.cljc; rg -n 'abandon|pending-request|address.*pending' src/cljc/dao/jing/content/step.cljc src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md | sed -n '330,730p'; rg -n 'make-acceptor|accept-step|mirror-step|dial\\\\*|defn dial' src/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc src/cljc/yin/repl/serve.cljc src/cljc/yin/repl/connect.cljc; rg -n 'abandon|pending-request|address.*pending' src/cljc/dao/jing/content/step.cljc src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"   330\t**The snapshot set** (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 7.2, amended) is: the\n   331\tmanifest the directory's HEAD names; every index-kind load that is\n   332\t`:loaded`; and **the installed head of each followed principal**, read\n   333\tfrom the node's local store, where every blob of it already is. The\n   334\tfollower records the installed manifests on the node value;\n   335\t`yin.vm.linker.dht/snapshots` reads them. Resolution (7.3) is otherwise\n   336\tunchanged.\n   337\t\n   338\t- The reader's own HEAD is never written by following.\n   339\t- Installation replaces the principal's installed manifest in one step.\n   340\t  No fold sees two heads of one principal, or none.\n   341\t\n   342\t**A silent publisher** changes nothing. The follower records the node\n   343\ttick reading at which its source last answered, as data for a drive\n   344\tthat wants to judge liveness with `dao.lease`. Nothing here reads it.\n   345\t\n   346\t### 5.6 When `(require 'alib)` refreshes (decision 6)\n   347\t\n   348\t**On a head event.** A require never asks the network for a newer head.\n   349\t\n   350\t- A `require` that links resolves against the snapshot set as it stands.\n   351\t- **A name may change meaning between two requires**: in two sessions,\n   352\t  or before and after `(reset)`. The require's provenance carries the\n   353\t  snapshot vector.\n   354\t- **Dependency mismatches surface sooner.** When `base` is republished\n   355\t  and a reader follows both `base` and `app`, `app`'s require refuses\n   356\t  `:yin.link.dht/dependency-binding` with `:mismatch` (7.4) until `app`\n   357\t  is republished. Following does not create this; it stops hiding it.\n   358\t- **First contact.** A require whose name is `:absent` while some\n   359\t  followed principal has no installed head parks pending on that\n   360\t  principal (R10) and is re-checked when its head is installed (R11's\n   361\t  shape). `(abandon)` and the link policy end it.\n   362\t- `(yin.head/heads)` answers, per principal, what is installed, the\n   363\t  candidate and its state, and the last refusal.\n   364\t\n   365\t**Pinning.** `--dht-manifest <address>` is unchanged: it hydrates an\n   366\tempty directory from that manifest, one-shot and never saved. A run\n   367\tgiven it follows nothing: with a saved `--dht-follow`, the pin wins for\n   368\tthat run and the banner says following is suspended. A direct entry\n   369\t(`--link-name my.lib=<address>`) still pins one name.\n   370\t\n   371\t### 5.7 Persistence and relink (decision 7)\n   372\t\n   373\t**Persistence.** The follower's durable state is the last installed\n   374\ttrace of each followed principal, verbatim, in `<node dir>/heads.edn`:\n   375\t\n   376\t```clojure\n   377\t{:version 1\n   378\t :heads {\"ed25519:<hex>\" {:yin.head/envelope {...} :yin.head/proof {...}}}}\n   379\t```\n   380\t\n   381\t- The whole signed trace is kept, not a manifest and a number: the floor\n   382\t  and the manifest are read from evidence that verifies again.\n   383\t- **Durable before used.** A confirmed head is installed in this order:\n   384\t  1. The follower emits `:confirmed` with the trace.\n   385\t  2. The owner replaces `heads.edn` atomically with that trace in its\n   386\t     principal's place.\n   387\t  3. Only when the write has returned does the owner call\n   388\t     `(head/install follower node principal trace)`: the head enters the\n   389\t     snapshot set, the floor moves, `:installed` is emitted.\n   390\t- Nothing reads a confirmed head before step 3.\n   391\t- A crash before step 2 restarts at the previous head, which is the last\n   392\t  one ever used. A crash after it restarts at the new head, whose blobs\n   393\t  are local. No crash restarts below a head that was exposed.\n   394\t- **A write that fails installs nothing.** The owner reports\n   395\t  `:yin.head/unpersisted` with the host's reason, the previous head\n   396\t  stays installed, and the write is tried again after the node's repair\n   397\t  delay. A newer confirmed head replaces the waiting one.\n   398\t- A composition with no durable directory declares `:heads :volatile`\n   399\t  and calls `install` on `:confirmed`. Its floor does not survive a\n   400\t  restart, by its own declaration.\n   401\t- At startup each record is verified again, checked against the followed\n   402\t  set, and its index walked in the local store. A record that fails\n   403\t  refuses startup naming it; nothing falls back to no floor.\n   404\t- An index loaded by hand stays process state, as now.\n   405\t\n   406\tRejected: recording observed traces as datoms in the reader's own index\n   407\t(two nodes following each other would move each other's HEAD forever).\n   408\t\n   409\t**Relink: a session keeps what it linked; `(reset)` leads to the new\n   410\tmodule.** `yin.vm` learns nothing, and a repeat `(require 'alib)` of a\n   411\tlinked name is what it is today. When an installed head makes a linked\n   412\tname resolve elsewhere, the shell prints one line: the name, both\n   413\taddresses, the principal and sequence, and that `(reset)` then\n   414\t`(require ...)` links it. The set is derived (the registry against the\n   415\tfold) and stored nowhere; `(yin.head/moved)` answers it.\n   416\t\n   417\t### 5.8 Command line and saved state (decision 8)\n   418\t\n   419\t- **The token** is `yin:<host:port>/<principal>`, printed once when both\n   420\t  the socket and the board endpoint are bound. A token with a third part\n   421\t  is refused: a manifest is a pin and belongs to `--dht-manifest`.\n   422\t- **`dht join <token>`** becomes `--index-store dht:<dir> --dht-peer\n   423\t  <host:port> --dht-principal <hex> --dht-follow <hex>@<host:port>`. It\n   424\t  passes no `--dht-manifest`: its first manifest is the first trace it\n   425\t  installs. The shell admits evaluation at once and says, per followed\n   426\t  principal, whether a head is installed.\n   427\t- **`state.edn`** keeps flags, and gains `--dht-follow` (repeatable).\n   428\t  It never holds a head. `--dht-manifest` stays one-shot.\n   429\t- **Hydration is not removed and `--dht-recover` is not added.** The\n   430\t  defect was joining by hydration (D1), and join no longer does. What\n   431\t  `--dht-manifest` does is still right for its two remaining uses: a\n   432\t  reproducible run in a fresh directory, and a publisher restoring a\n   433\t  lost directory under a kept key, which is `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 6.5's\n   434\t  documented remedy. Leaving it alone keeps that remedy with no\n   435\t  amendment and no new flag.\n   436\t- A directory hydrated in an earlier run and following in a later one\n   437\t  folds both snapshots. That is correct (the fold deduplicates\n   438\t  envelopes and honours retractions) and is stated, not prevented.\n   439\t- If the TCP port of the same number cannot be bound, the node serves\n   440\t  no board, prints no token and names the port; the operator chooses\n   441\t  another `--dht-port`.\n   442\t\n   443\t### 5.9 Failure and security (decision 9)\n   444\t\n   445\t- A trace is honoured only when it verifies under a declared key. Where\n   446\t  it was read is never evidence.\n   447\t- A head is installed only after its index is local, its sequence is\n   448\t  confirmed by that index, and its trace is durable.\n   449\t- The floor never moves down and moves only at installation.\n   450\t- No failure retracts an installed head, and none waits on a clock.\n   451\t- **Nothing above mentions loopback.** Loopback bounds who can connect.\n   452\t  No rule trusts a source for being local (section 8.4).\n   453\t\n   454\tSection 10 is the table.\n   455\t\n   456\t### 5.10 Portability (decision 10)\n   457\t\n   458\tPortable `.cljc`: the trace, `judge`, the follower, the board, the\n   459\trendering of `heads.edn`, and the ws composition (`dao.stream.ws`,\n   460\t`dao.stream.ws-project`, `dao.stream.remote`). Host seams, all existing:\n   461\tEd25519 (`yin.vm.linker.sign`), the WebSocket listener and dialer\n   462\t(`src/clj/dao/stream/ws/jvm.clj`, `src/cljs/dao/stream/ws/node.cljs`,\n   463\t`src/cljd/dao/stream/ws/dart.cljd`), the atomic file replace\n   464\t(`dao.space.store.fs`). A browser can dial but has no Ed25519 here: it\n   465\tfollows nothing yet.\n   466\t\n   467\tClojureDart traps: `heads.edn` is rendered with no whitespace before a\n   468\tclosing bracket; host branches put `:cljd` first; no protocol method\n   469\ttakes `[_ _]`; no test reaches a private var by `#'`; the sequence stays\n   470\twithin 2^53 and the vectors include one above 2^32; a manifest address\n   471\tfrom the wire is compared with `=`; a trace decoded from the channel\n   472\tcodec is verified over `dao.jing/canonical-bytes`, asserted on all\n   473\tthree hosts.\n   474\t\n   475\t## 6. The plain Clojure API\n   476\t\n   477\t```text\n   478\tjoin -> publish! -> assert! -> announce! -> deposit!        (publisher)\n   479\tjoin -> follow -> attach -> step ... -> names -> link       (reader)\n   480\t```\n   481\t\n   482\t<table>\n   483\t<tr><th>Function</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>\n   484\t<tr><td><code>(yin.vm.linker.head/verify trace)</code></td>\n   485\t<td>True or false; never throws.</td></tr>\n   486\t<tr><td><code>(head/seq-of datoms)</code></td>\n   487\t<td>The greatest <code>t</code>; <code>nil</code> for no datoms.</td></tr>\n   488\t<tr><td><code>(head/judge principal floor installed trace)</code></td>\n   489\t<td>The rule of 5.5, as data.</td></tr>\n   490\t<tr><td><code>(head/board)</code>,\n   491\t<code>(head/deposit! board key manifest datoms)</code></td>\n   492\t<td>The capacity-1 ring, and sign-and-append; refuses\n   493\t<code>:yin.head/seq-collision</code>, <code>:seq-regression</code>,\n   494\t<code>:empty</code>, <code>:no-key</code>.</td></tr>\n   495\t<tr><td><code>(head/follow node opts)</code></td>\n   496\t<td>A follower: <code>:follow</code> principals, <code>:heads</code> the\n   497\tpersisted records or <code>:volatile</code>.</td></tr>\n   498\t<tr><td><code>(head/attach follower principal reader)</code></td>\n   499\t<td>Give the follower a <code>dao.stream</code> reader handle for that\n   500\tprincipal's board. Any handle.</td></tr>\n   501\t<tr><td><code>(head/step follower node now)</code></td>\n   502\t<td>One pass after <code>dao.space.dht/step</code>:\n   503\t<code>[follower node events]</code>. It confirms; it never installs.\n   504\t</td></tr>\n   505\t<tr><td><code>(head/install follower node principal trace)</code></td>\n   506\t<td>Step 3 of 5.7. Refused unless that trace is the confirmed one.</td>\n   507\t</tr>\n   508\t<tr><td><code>(head/heads follower)</code>,\n   509\t<code>(head/records follower principal trace)</code></td>\n   510\t<td>The status of 5.6, and the value <code>heads.edn</code> holds once\n   511\tthat trace is written.</td></tr>\n   512\t<tr><td><code>(head/moved names registry)</code></td>\n   513\t<td>The linked names whose resolved address differs.</td></tr>\n   514\t<tr><td><code>yin.vm.linker.head.ws/serve</code>, <code>serve-step</code>,\n   515\t<code>dial</code>, <code>dial-step</code></td>\n   516\t<td>The board's acceptor and the reader's dial, thin compositions of\n   517\t<code>dao.stream.ws-project</code>; the listener and the attacher are\n   518\targuments. The only namespace here that knows a transport.</td></tr>\n   519\t</table>\n   520\t\n   521\t`dao.space.dht` gains `abandon` and the kind-conflict refusal, and\n   522\tlearns nothing about heads. Events are maps under `:yin.head/event`:\n   523\t`:confirmed`, `:installed`, `:refused` (a reason and the trace),\n   524\t`:unloadable`, `:source-lost`. `yin.repl` adds a `yin.head` host module,\n   525\t`heads` and `moved`, each one call into the functions above; the shell\n   526\towns the file, the lines, the flags, and the dial.\n   527\t\n   528\t## 7. Limits and defaults\n   529\t\n   530\t<table>\n   531\t<tr><th>Datum</th><th>Default</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>\n   532\t<tr><td>Board capacity</td><td>1</td><td>Fixed.</td></tr>\n   533\t<tr><td>Candidates per principal</td><td>1</td><td>Fixed (5.5).</td></tr>\n   534\t<tr><td><code>:head-poll-ticks</code></td><td>5000</td>\n   535\t<td>Node ticks between two <code>next</code> calls on one source. It\n   536\tbounds how often a reader asks, not how late it learns.</td></tr>\n   537\t<tr><td>Retry delays (load, write, re-dial)</td>\n   538\t<td><code>:repair-ticks</code> to <code>:repair-max-ticks</code></td>\n   539\t<td>Doubling, the node's existing bounds.</td></tr>\n   540\t<tr><td>Followed principals</td><td>at most 64</td>\n   541\t<td>More refuses startup.</td></tr>\n   542\t<tr><td>Board endpoint slots</td><td>8</td>\n   543\t<td>The acceptor's handoff slots, as the REPL endpoint's.</td></tr>\n   544\t</table>\n   545\t\n   546\tFollowing never makes the node `busy?`.\n   547\t\n   548\t## 8. Cross-machine: the owner's question\n   549\t\n   550\t> if the abstraction is dao.stream, cross-machine would not require a\n   551\t> complete redesign, right?\n   552\t\n   553\t**Right.** The core is built so that it is true, and the one place it\n   554\twould have been false is removed. Following across machines changes how\n   555\ta reader handle is obtained and who may connect to the board. It changes\n   556\tno trace, no rule, no file and no reader semantics.\n   557\t\n   558\t### 8.1 What is transport-agnostic, and stays so\n   559\t\n   560\t<table>\n   561\t<tr><th>Part</th><th>Why it cannot depend on a transport</th></tr>\n   562\t<tr><td>The trace and its signature (5.2)</td>\n   563\t<td>No address, no time, no channel datum is signed.</td></tr>\n   564\t<tr><td><code>judge</code> (5.5)</td>\n   565\t<td>No source argument. One source is the degenerate case.</td></tr>\n   566\t<tr><td>Confirmation (5.2, 5.5)</td>\n   567\t<td>The index is content-addressed and fetched by the DHT, which\n   568\tverifies every blob against its address.</td></tr>\n   569\t<tr><td>Persistence (5.7)</td>\n   570\t<td><code>heads.edn</code> holds signed traces and no source.</td></tr>\n   571\t<tr><td>The follower (5.3, 5.5)</td>\n   572\t<td>It holds a reader handle and calls <code>cursor</code> and\n   573\t<code>next</code>. A lost source is one event, whatever lost it.</td>\n   574\t</tr>\n   575\t<tr><td>Reader semantics (5.6, 5.7)</td>\n   576\t<td>They start at the snapshot set.</td></tr>\n   577\t</table>\n   578\t\n   579\tOnly `yin.vm.linker.head.ws` and the shell's flags know a transport.\n   580\tSlice H1 proves the claim by test: the follower is driven to completion\n   581\tover a plain local ring, with no channel at all.\n   582\t\n   583\t### 8.2 What each later step adds and amends\n   584\t\n   585\t<table>\n   586\t<tr><th>Step</th><th>Adds</th><th>Amends</th>\n   587\t<th>Changes the core's trace, rule or persistence?</th></tr>\n   588\t<tr><td><strong>Serving off loopback</strong> (8.3)</td>\n   589\t<td>A bind that is not loopback; a bound on concurrent sessions; a\n   590\tbanner line.</td>\n   591\t<td>This document 5.1 and 5.8; <code>yin.vm.linker.head.ws</code>;\n   592\t<code>yin.repl.main</code>. Nothing in <code>dao.stream.*</code> if the\n   593\tunverified items of 8.3 hold.</td>\n   594\t<td><strong>No.</strong></td></tr>\n   595\t<tr><td><strong>Relaying</strong></td>\n   596\t<td>A second ring per followed principal holding the installed trace\n   597\tverbatim, read from <code>heads.edn</code>; a flag.</td>\n   598\t<td><code>dao.stream.remote.md</code> 2 and 5: C3 must be settled,\n   599\tbecause one name then reaches many rings (bless service names, or a\n   600\tnamed <code>descriptor</code> request that answers the ring's own\n   601\tdescriptor). <code>dao.stream.remote</code> if the second.</td>\n   602\t<td><strong>No.</strong> It is why the trace carries no address and\n   603\twhy <code>heads.edn</code> keeps the whole signed trace.</td></tr>\n   604\t<tr><td><strong>Many sources per principal</strong></td>\n   605\t<td>A cursor per source; a scheduling policy for which candidate loads\n   606\twhen sources disagree.</td>\n   607\t<td>This document 5.5 \"The candidate\" and section 7; the follower's\n   608\tprocess state and the shape <code>heads</code> answers.</td>\n   609\t<td><strong>No</strong> for the trace, <code>judge</code>, the floor\n   610\tand <code>heads.edn</code>. <strong>Yes for one plumbing rule:</strong>\n   611\t\"latest observed wins\" is sound only for one writer's ring. Many\n   612\tsources need \"greatest verified sequence, with a fair turn for an\n   613\tunloadable one\", which is where the two scheduling defects of the\n   614\tsecond review live. That design starts from those findings.</td></tr>\n   615\t<tr><td><strong>UDP on the DHT socket</strong></td>\n   616\t<td>A mirror on the node's datagram socket; a return-path proof.</td>\n   617\t<td><code>dao.jing.dht.md</code> 3 and 8 (frozen);\n   618\t<code>dao.stream.remote.md</code> 3.2; <code>dao.stream.udp</code>:\n   619\tthe proof must ride the outer fragment envelope and be checked before\n   620\treassembly, because a fragment carries no request key\n   621\t(<code>src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:135-149, 312-325</code>);\n   622\t<code>dao.space.dht</code>.</td>\n   623\t<td><strong>No.</strong> It is the costliest step and touches the core\n   624\tnowhere.</td></tr>\n   625\t</table>\n   626\t\n   627\t**The redesign risks, found now.** Three, none in the core:\n   628\t\n   629\t1. C3 is unsettled in the contract and already depended on by\n   630\t   `yin.repl`. It blocks relaying, not cross-machine following.\n   631\t2. The single-candidate rule does not generalise to many sources. It is\n   632\t   one paragraph of 5.5, and it is labelled as such.\n   633\t3. The token assumes TCP and UDP share one external port number. Behind\n   634\t   a NAT that maps them apart it needs a second number. A third token\n   635\t   part of the form `ws:<port>` is reserved for that; it is additive.\n   636\t\n   637\t### 8.3 The first cross-machine transport: WebSocket\n   638\t\n   639\t<table>\n   640\t<tr><th></th><th><code>dao.stream.ws</code></th>\n   641\t<th>UDP on the DHT socket, plus a return-path proof</th></tr>\n   642\t<tr><td>Reflection of traffic at a spoofed source</td>\n   643\t<td>None: a WebSocket rides TCP, whose handshake is the return-path\n   644\tproof.</td>\n   645\t<td>The problem itself. Needs a new proof, on fragments too.</td></tr>\n   646\t<tr><td>Serving an arbitrary ring with existing code</td>\n   647\t<td><strong>Yes.</strong> <code>ws-project/make-acceptor</code> takes\n   648\tany mirror table and <code>accept-step!</code> runs\n   649\t<code>remote/mirror-step</code> per accepted connection\n   650\t(<code>ws_project.cljc:161-190, 248-262</code>);\n   651\t<code>dial</code>, <code>dial-attach!</code> and\n   652\t<code>dial-step!</code> give the reader a reflection\n   653\t(<code>305-420</code>). <code>yin.repl.serve</code> does exactly this\n   654\ttoday with a two-entry table (<code>serve.cljc:224-225, 291</code>).\n   655\t</td>\n   656\t<td>No. The node has no mirror on its socket; that is new code in\n   657\t<code>dao.space.dht</code>.</td></tr>\n   658\t<tr><td>Contracts amended</td><td>None expected.</td>\n   659\t<td>Two, one of them frozen.</td></tr>\n   660\t<tr><td>NAT</td>\n   661\t<td>One side must accept a TCP connection: a public address or a\n   662\tforwarded port. No hole punching. Either side may be the one that\n   663\tdials, so no node is privileged, but two peers that are both behind\n   664\tNATs need a relay pair\n   665\t(<code>dao.stream.remote.md</code> 3.3, 4).</td>\n   666\t<td>Hole punching is possible in principle, through a meeting peer.\n   667\tThe DHT has none today (<code>dao.jing.dht.md</code> section 6: \"NAT\n   668\tmeeting is not in this epic\").</td></tr>\n   669\t<tr><td>What the reader must already reach</td>\n   670\t<td colspan=\"2\">The publisher's UDP socket, to fetch blobs. With no NAT\n   671\tmeeting in the DHT, cross-machine use today already needs the publisher\n   672\tdirectly reachable. WebSocket asks for the same host on TCP as\n   673\twell.</td></tr>\n   674\t<tr><td>Token</td>\n   675\t<td><code>yin:&lt;host:port&gt;/&lt;principal&gt;</code>, the port\n   676\tnumber shared (5.1); <code>ws:&lt;port&gt;</code> reserved.</td>\n   677\t<td>The same two parts, one socket.</td></tr>\n   678\t<tr><td>Confidentiality</td>\n   679\t<td>None: <code>wss://</code> \"has no settled descriptor form\"\n   680\t(<code>src/cljc/yin/repl/connect.cljc:259</code>). The head is public\n   681\tand its integrity is the signature's.</td>\n   682\t<td>None either.</td></tr>\n   683\t</table>\n   684\t\n   685\t**Recommendation: WebSocket, for the core and for the first step off\n   686\tloopback.** It is the transport with a return path, it serves the ring\n   687\twith code that exists, and it amends no contract. UDP on the DHT socket\n   688\tstays the deferred alternative, wanted when hole-punched peers must\n   689\tfollow each other without a relay.\n   690\t\n   691\t**What that step is.** Remove the loopback condition of 5.1, and land\n   692\tthe checks below. No design beyond this section is expected.\n   693\t\n   694\t**Unverified, and to be verified by that step before it lands:**\n   695\t\n   696\t- That the acceptor bounds concurrent sessions. The handoff has 8\n   697\t  slots; whether `:sessions` is bounded under many held connections was\n   698\t  not checked.\n   699\t- That one connection cannot make a step long: `mirror-step` loops to\n   700\t  `blocked`, and the spec's \"composition budget\" was not found in it.\n   701\t- That the listener seams, written for `yin.repl.host`, compose outside\n   702\t  `yin.repl.serve` on all three hosts without moving code.\n   703\t- That a TCP listener binds at the UDP socket's number on each host,\n   704\t  including after an ephemeral UDP bind.\n   705\t\n   706\t### 8.4 The assumption cross-machine adds: locality is not trust\n   707\t\n   708\tOn loopback, whoever connects is on the machine. Off it, anyone is.\n   709\tNothing in the core relied on the first:\n   710\t\n   711\t- **Reading the board** was never restricted. A head is public wherever\n   712\t  its node is reachable, as its index is under `--dht-publish`.\n   713\t- **A hostile or intercepted source** can do three things: show a\n   714\t  forged trace (`:bad-proof`), show an old one (`:stale` against a\n   715\t  durable floor), or show nothing. `judge` already treats every source\n   716\t  that way; it has no notion of a local one.\n   717\t- **First contact has no floor**, on loopback or off. Off loopback an\n   718\t  interceptor on a cleartext channel can show a first-time reader an\n   719\t  old valid head, or withhold. That limit was always in section 10; the\n   720\t  network makes it reachable. `wss` or a second source narrows it;\n   721\t  neither is in the core.\n   722\t- **The index** is fetched by content address and confirmed against the\n   723\t  trace.\n   724\t- **What does change** is resource exposure: connections, sessions,\n   725\t  step time. Those are the unverified items of 8.3, and they are the\n   726\t  whole of the work.\n   727\t\n   728\t## 9. What is rejected\n   729\t\n   730\t- **A head under a DHT key.** C2; it is the mutable-pointer service.\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/serve.cljc:17:   `dao.stream.ws-project/accept-step!` (which itself adopts every accepted\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/serve.cljc:176:   channel ring `dao.stream.ws-project/accept-step!` projects deposited\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/serve.cljc:177:   payload onto and mirror-steps against.  One client's eviction pressure\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/serve.cljc:288:            acceptor (ws-project/make-acceptor\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/serve.cljc:696:              _ (ws-project/accept-step! (:acceptor endpoint) now)\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc:20:   over real attachments. The accepting peer drives `accept-step!`:\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc:24:   reader and the socket handle, dao.stream.remote/mirror-step. The\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc:161:(defn make-acceptor\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc:248:(defn accept-step!\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc:273:             (remote/mirror-step (:table @acceptor)\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc:305:(defn dial\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc:348:(defn dial-attach!\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc:388:(defn dial-reflect!\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc:403:(defn dial-step!\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc:416:             (fn [c] (remote/mirror-step table ring c handle))))\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc:1297:              (:requested :pending-request)\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/content/step.cljc:6:   request-put / request-get / request-materialize / step / abandon\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/content/step.cljc:53:   abandon retires only the retained :unsent request -- the one delivery\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/content/step.cljc:104:(def abandoned-code\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/content/step.cljc:105:  \"The default abandon reason: this layer's own word for a request given\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/content/step.cljc:107:  :dao.jing.content/abandoned)\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/content/step.cljc:225:        {:outcome :pending-request\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/content/step.cljc:641:;; step and abandon\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/content/step.cljc:715:  ([state id] (retire state id abandoned-code))\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/content/step.cljc:736:(defn abandon\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/content/step.cljc:738:   layer's abandoned-code): the composition that is about to change what\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/content/step.cljc:743:   Records with nothing on the wire are untouched: abandon retires the\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/content/step.cljc:746:  ([state] (abandon state abandoned-code))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc | sed -n '1255,1328p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing/content/step.cljc | sed -n '200,232p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc | sed -n '161,195p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc | sed -n '248,280p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/repl/connect.cljc | sed -n '64,82p'; rg -n 'load-index|load-module|catch|refused' src/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc | tail -24; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc | sed -n '1160,1171p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '275,285p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '155,163p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
